 {:id :host-dependent-expectation
  :title "the file's expectation depends on the host's C library: what NULL expands to is implementation-defined"
  :kind :na
  :why "NULL is an implementation-defined null pointer constant ([support.types.nullptr]/2, C's 7.21/3): glibc's and gcc's ginclude stddef.h define it as __null, musl's (x86_64-linux-musl, C's own target, c/README.md) as nullptr from C++11, so whether a conversion from NULL to an integer is a warning or an error is the host's, not the standard's: no expected output a conforming front end owes (queue 499, DECISION 499-2 (a)); before, :musl-libc's"
  :decided "499-2 (a) (the main session, 2026-11-20, under the user's rule of 2026-11-20); queue 502"
  :why-each {"gxx-dg/template/nontype10.C" "expects a warning naming NULL at line 8 for `A<NULL>`, an int template argument (glibc's __null converts with -Wconversion-null's warning), and an error at line 9 for `B<NULL>`, a void* argument (__null is no null pointer value of pointer type there); with musl's NULL, nullptr, line 8 is an error and line 9 none (g++ 15.2 itself here: test/cpp/ref-dg-gxx15.edn's {8 1}), as Arbace's front end gives"}
  :tests ["gxx-dg/template/nontype10.C"]
  :match []}
