 {:id :musl-libc
  :title "the target's C library is musl: g++.dg's expectation is glibc's (NULL as __null, iconv failing on an unencodable character)"
  :kind :design
  :why "on x86_64-linux-musl (C's own target, c/README.md) g++'s #include <stddef.h> and <cstddef> find musl's header (/usr/include/stddef.h: `#define NULL nullptr` from C++11), not gcc's ginclude/stddef.h (`#define NULL __null`), so the -Wconversion-null tests' warnings on an integer from NULL are errors, g++ 15.2's own here (\"cannot convert 'std::nullptr_t' to 'int'\") as clang's (warn/Wnull-conversion-1.C, warn/Wconversion-null-4.C; template/nontype10.C since 499-2 (a) :host-dependent-expectation's, out of the judge's cases); and musl's iconv converts a character the charset cannot encode to '*' instead of failing (EILSEQ), so -fexec-charset=ISO-8859-1's \"converting to execution character set\" errors are not given by g++ here either (cpp26/literals2.C; dg-require-iconv only asks that iconv_open succeeds). The reference errs as the target does, not as the directives say. Since cpp-read (2026-11-22) the own front end converts to ISO-8859-1 itself (-fexec-charset, arbace.repr.cpp.native), its errors the directives', so literals2.C passes by the own verdict and left the class"
  :decided "cpp-compl (2026-11-08): found among gxx-disagrees' files by g++'s reference"
  :tests ["gxx-dg/warn/Wnull-conversion-1.C" "gxx-dg/warn/Wconversion-null-4.C"]
  :match []}
